Allow an exact pixel distance in a gesture's distance field.

lisgd's distance guard has three settings and they are thirds of the screen:
Short, Medium at 33%, Long at 66%. On a 1920x1200 output that means a downward
swipe can be guarded at "any distance at all" or "at least 396px", and at
nothing in between.

That gap is not academic. Close-window wants a guard, being the only destructive
gesture here, and measuring real attempts put them at 262-309px -- comfortably
deliberate, and comfortably under Medium. The gesture was recognised perfectly
every time, correct direction and correct edge, and thrown away on distance;
the only alternative was to drop the guard entirely and let any flick near the
top edge close a window.

So the distance field now also takes a number, meaning that many pixels of
travel: `1,UD,T,180,R,swaymsg kill`. S/M/L keep working and are unchanged.

The measured distance has to be captured in touchup() before resetslot() wipes
the coordinates, since the release gesture is not dispatched until every finger
is up. The new struct field goes last because the defaults in config.h are
positional initialisers -- anywhere earlier shifts all of them, while at the end
they simply leave it zero-filled, which is the "unused" value.

Applies on top of lisgd-export-gesture-coords.patch,
lisgd-cardinals-before-diagonals.patch and lisgd-pressed-decline.patch.

--- a/lisgd.c
+++ b/lisgd.c
@@ -78,6 +78,16 @@
